00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.090
Look, reinforcement learning “is so damn
inefficient”.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:36.870
For it to find the right solution for a problem,
it starts from scratch to search for the solution

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.910
and only gets sparse / infrequent and late
reward.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:46.209
Even worse, there are usually many possible
ways to do things until the problem gets solved

00:02:46.209 --> 00:02:48.360
– and most are wrong.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:54.790
And the game of Stratego is one hell of an
example of a huge search space: A Stratego

00:02:54.790 --> 00:03:00.209
game takes around 1000 turns, which is an
order of magnitude longer than the game of

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:03.480
Go which takes around 300 turns.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.099
Even worse, one player does not have full
information about what is happening on the

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:09.400
opponent’s side.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.770
This, on top of the fact that the number of
possible game states is astronomical with

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:20.740
10^535 ways of arranging pieces.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:29.580
In comparison, the number of 10^360 game states
of Go or the 10^300 estimated ways to fold

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.209
a protein, look like small numbers.

00:03:32.209 --> 00:03:35.480
The numbers for poker and chess are even smaller.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:41.920
While in games like Chess and Go, all games
start from the same configuration, in Stratego

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.909
each game starts differently: Through a deployment
phase, the players position their 40 pieces

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:50.110
of different rank and function onto the board.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:54.530
There are 10^66 starting configurations here.

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:59.140
Each player does not know anything about the
configuration of the enemy pieces.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:05.700
They can only find out what a piece from the
other player is when two pieces meet on the

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:06.760
battlefield.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:12.150
Among these pieces that the players have placed,
there is a flag piece, and the goal of the

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:16.220
game is to capture the opponent’s flag to
win the game.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:22.440
Of course, the other pieces defend this flag,
and the opponents must capture the other player’s

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.419
pieces to reach the flag.

00:04:24.419 --> 00:04:30.680
Stratego is a so-called zero-sum game, because
the reward for one player – the capture

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:35.169
of a piece –, is a minus rewards, so a loss,
for the other player, since they have lost

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:36.169
a piece.

00:04:36.169 --> 00:04:40.990
Okay, so Stratego is a hard, long game of
incomplete information with a lot of possible

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.020
states.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:47.220
Then why use reinforcement learning to learn
it from scratch with DeepNash instead of using

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.330
other ways to program a Stratego bot?

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:57.980
Well, one of the reasons is that even though
the ideas behind it and its training procedure

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:05.420
might apply only to other two-player zero-sum
games, the author argue that it could help

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:11.560
researchers find a way to apply what they
learned here to, we cite: „crowd and traffic

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:16.760
modeling, smart grid,
auction design, and market problems”.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:24.039
The cool thing about DeepNash is that the
ideas behind it are new for RL, and it is

00:05:24.039 --> 00:05:30.190
not yet another RL system like we have seen
with AlphaGo or AlphaZero.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:37.449
With Go and other board games, the RL agents
could observe everything about the game as

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:40.500
there was no hidden information from the agent.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:47.060
Also, the state space was not as large as
for Stratego, so the agent could perform Monte

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:53.270
Carlo tree search, which is just a fancy name
for saying that the agent can simulate future

00:05:53.270 --> 00:06:00.350
states and rank them based on whether they
lead to good or bad outcomes and choose the

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:01.350
best.

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:07.860
But to play Stratego, DeepNash has the problem
that its state space is so huge that it is

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:13.620
unfeasible for Monte Carlo tree search and,
it cannot observe the identity of the pieces

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.240
of the enemy on the board.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.460
Therefore, it cannot model the enemy’s behavior
very well.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:24.900
Ok, so if it cannot do that well, then let’s
renounce this idea altogether and do a so-called

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.210
model-free reinforcement learning agent.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:32.810
Model-free is again just a fancy name
for saying that it does not have an explicit

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:38.560
belief space tracking and modelling what the
enemy does, so it does not calculate probabilities

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:40.470
for the opponent’s states.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:47.319
Instead, it focuses entirely on its own play
to steer it towards a Nash equilibrium.

00:06:47.319 --> 00:06:48.979
A, what!?

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:51.400
Again, a fancy word for saying that

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:58.979
DeepNash’s way of playing converges to strategies
that are very hard to exploit by an opponent

00:06:58.979 --> 00:07:01.330
who has equal or lower skill.

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:07.110
If it plays against a version of the same
skill as it is, then the chances of losing

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:10.350
or winning on both sides are equal, thus 50%.

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:16.419
So, the idea here is to focus on its own play
to develop unexploitable strategies for the

00:07:16.419 --> 00:07:17.419
opponent.

00:07:17.419 --> 00:07:21.620
Unexploitable can mean that it plays in a
way that is hard to predict so an opponent

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:23.120
cannot see a pattern.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.379
And it learns to reach Nash equilibrium, by
playing against itself and you know, if you

00:07:27.379 --> 00:07:32.870
let an AI play against its own version and
to learn to find unexploitable strategies,

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:38.960
then it eventually becomes so good, that it
becomes very, very robust in a way that humans

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.660
become very unlikely to exploit its play.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:46.090
Now, let’s break down the technical details
of this paper and explain it into more detail

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:53.889
how to implement R-NaD which is short for
Regularized Nash Dynamics with neural networks

00:07:53.889 --> 00:07:59.830
an RL agent that converges to Nash equilibrium
for Stratego.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:04.930
I hope Ms. Coffee Bean got as many of these
details right, because honestly, the technical

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:09.240
details are not the clearest in the paper,
especially since a lot of what is needed to

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:13.949
understand DeepNash is scattered around in
the supplementary material.

00:08:13.949 --> 00:08:21.430
We’ll give it a go: the authors train DeepNash
by letting it play Stratego against itself.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:29.310
Its input consists of a tensor representation
of the board game and 40 past states of it.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:36.909
Then a large U-Net processes this input and
four smaller U-Nets act as different network

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:41.560
heads, using what the large one produces,
to make decisions.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:47.180
They do not really make decisions, but they
rather predict probabilities, or fitness for

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:48.180
actions.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:53.450
The first U-Net is responsible for the deployment
phase of the Stratego game.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:09:00.170
The player needs to place 40 pieces, so in
40 steps at each board position, it predicts

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:03.820
the probabilities for each piece to be chosen
there.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:10.019
The second U-Net acts during the game phase
of Stratego and selects the piece to be played

00:09:10.019 --> 00:09:11.660
next by DeepNash.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:14.860
In other words, each piece gets a probability
to be played.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:20.590
The third neural net head decides how to move
the selected piece by the second head.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:25.610
It predicts probabilities for a piece to execute
each allowed move, where move could also mean

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:30.450
that it would attack an enemy piece if it
decides to move the piece on a field occupied

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.370
by an enemy piece.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:37.600
And the last U-Net is the one responsible
for predicting the value, which is the long-time

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.899
reward for the game.

00:09:39.899 --> 00:09:45.519
DeepNash makes a move in Stratego as follows:
We have these heads that predict probabilities

00:09:45.519 --> 00:09:47.030
for actions.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:52.090
To make actions during training and inference,
we simply sample from the predicted probability

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:53.090
distributions.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:58.519
This means that often, the most probable piece
or actions is chosen, but sometimes, also

00:09:58.519 --> 00:10:01.260
low probability choices can be sampled.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:08.450
This helps with the unpredictability of DeepNash’s
play to make it hard to exploit by an opponent.

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:12.980
Now, let’s get to explaining the training
of DeepNash.

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:17.810
It is time for the game theory part, that
describes how this neural architecture that

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:24.959
we just described, converges to Nash equilibrium,
which means that it develops an unexploitable

00:10:24.959 --> 00:10:25.959
playstyle.

00:10:25.959 --> 00:10:34.279
Well, by implementing the Regularized Nash
Dynamics algorithm, or R-NaD in short.

00:10:34.279 --> 00:10:36.829
All is described by these formulas.

00:10:36.829 --> 00:10:40.410
Great, now that everyone got it, let’s move
on.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:45.960
Kidding, let’s explain the formula in simple
words and break it down.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:52.220
As we are used to in reinforcement learning,
the agent – here DeepNash – takes feedback

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:56.079
through a so-called reward from the game.

00:10:56.079 --> 00:10:58.639
The higher the reward, the better it did.

00:10:58.639 --> 00:11:04.070
A low reward tells it to update and do better
at the next game.

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:08.290
The highest reward for Stratego of course,
is when DeepNash takes over the enemy flag

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:09.990
since this wins the game.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:16.350
But there are smaller rewards, like uncovering
the identity or taking over enemy pieces.

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:22.330
Stratego is a zero-sum game, so DeepNash receives
negative reward, thus feedback when its opponent

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:27.350
obtains positive reward, because this means
that DeepNash just lost a piece if the enemy

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.350
got the same, but positive reward.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:34.420
Okay, but it seems like the game reward alone
is not enough to reinforce DeepNash to reach

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:40.380
Nash equilibrium, but a reward transformation
through policy regularization.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:46.760
Let’s break this down: To compute the reward
for DeepNash, the authors take the game reward

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:52.700
from DeepNash’es action this step and add
a new term to it [the log] which is higher

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:58.600
when DeepNash has a similar policy to a previous
version of itself.

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:05.370
Policy is again a fancy RL word for saying
the probability / quality of each action.

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:13.570
So, if DeepNash makes similar decision to
its previous version, gets raised, otherwise

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:14.570
diminished.

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:19.550
Based on this reward, the authors apply the
“replicator dynamics”, which is a fancy

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:26.310
way to say that they update DeepNash’s parameters
so it can predict the action probabilities

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:29.519
better in the future as follows:

00:12:29.519 --> 00:12:36.170
if an action performed better than the estimated
average over all actions, they increased its

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:39.180
probability and decreased it otherwise.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:45.149
In this way, they reinforce actions of high
fitness and decrease the probability of low

00:12:45.149 --> 00:12:46.959
fitness actions.

00:12:46.959 --> 00:12:53.190
And game theory tells us if this replicator
dynamics update is applied iteratively, it

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:58.660
will converge to a fixed point, where the
update rule, when applied, does not change

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:03.150
the policy, so the predicted action probabilities
anymore.

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:07.970
And after the policy here does not change
anymore, it is ready to go back into the reward

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:13.890
transformation and the reward is adapted based
on this new estimated policy.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:19.240
Then the whole process of the replicator dynamics
repeats, a new fixed point for the policy

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:24.800
is found, and so on, until finally, DeepNash
reaches Nash equilibrium, which is guaranteed

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:26.910
theoretically to be reached.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:32.780
So far, all the applied procedures where game
theoretically founded for any two-player zero-sum

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:33.780
game.

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:38.399
So, if the reward here would not be the one
from Stratego rules, but some other one, this

00:13:38.399 --> 00:13:40.420
procedure would apply there too.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:43.750
In other words, R-NaD is Stratego-agnostic.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:50.779
But to make DeepNash definitely wins, the
authors need to apply some Stratego-specific

00:13:50.779 --> 00:13:53.880
hacks,
and this is maybe a point where their collaborator

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:59.690
Vincent de Boer who is a former Stratego world
champion, could help the most.

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:05.720
Because DeepNash predicts the policy, thus
probabilities for each action by using a softmax,

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:10.850
there are many actions with low probability
that is nonetheless not zero.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:14.910
And to do its next move, DeepNash samples
from this probability distribution, so it

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:21.040
is possible, though very, very rarely, that
it samples such a bad action with low probability.

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:26.399
Therefore, the authors further finetune DeepNash
by zero-ing probabilities under a certain

00:14:26.399 --> 00:14:34.230
threshold and discretize the probabilities
that could be any float number into rational

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:35.970
numbers.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:41.790
Ask Ms. Coffee Bean why this discretization
is needed, because I do not know.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.420
Maybe someone enlightens us in the comments?

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:47.740
Anyway, this was the training of the model
and its hacks.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:52.300
But the author’s tricks are not exhausted
yet, since they apply one more thing during

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:57.820
inference only to make sure that DeepNash
does not do stupid mistakes: they apply some

00:14:57.820 --> 00:15:02.779
ideas that humans have come up to remove actions
that are obviously mistakes.

00:15:02.779 --> 00:15:04.980
And how well does DeepNash do?

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:06.820
Quite well, thanks for asking.

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:13.940
It had a 97% win rate over 800 games against
other Stratego bots.

00:15:13.940 --> 00:15:19.829
Against humans, it had a win rate of 84% and
was third on the Gravon games platform as

00:15:19.829 --> 00:15:21.970
of April 2022.

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:26.079
But its behavior was more interesting than
the actual numbers.

00:15:26.079 --> 00:15:31.970
DeepNash was trained for Nash equilibrium,
so reached a strategy hard to exploit.

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:37.089
Namely, to be unexploitable, it played in
a way that is hard to predict so an opponent

00:15:37.089 --> 00:15:38.660
cannot see a pattern.

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:43.200
It also understood that it is important to
find information about the enemy pieces and

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:49.860
sacrificed two important pieces, a 7 and an
8 to locate high pieces from the opponent,

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:52.410
as you can see here in this game state.

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:53.700
It won.

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:55.220
It also learned how to bluff.

00:15:55.220 --> 00:16:00.509
In this example in the blog post from DeepMind,
we see how it uses the two, which is a weak

00:16:00.509 --> 00:16:06.280
piece as if it were a stronger piece, because
it pursues the 8 of the opponent.

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:11.300
The opponent thinks it is a high piece that
DeepNash is moving there, so it uses its Spy,

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:14.310
a valuable piece and loses it.

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:18.990
So not only has DeepNash succeeded to win
at this game against other bots and humans,

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:23.190
but it learned how to bluff, which is highly
impressive if you ask Ms. Coffee Bean.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:28.180
What do you think is the next game to be taken
over by AI?

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:30.300
Let us know in the comments.

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:31.690
Thanks for watching and see you next time!

00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:31.740
Okay, bye!

